From: Marcus Ramberg <marcus@means.no>
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: qcom: lpass-lpi: defer probe when the clocks time out

The LPASS LPI pinctrl core and audio clocks are provided by the ADSP,
through q6prm over GLINK. If the pinctrl driver probes before the ADSP
remoteproc has booted, clk_bulk_prepare_enable() does not return
-EPROBE_DEFER: the clock enable request goes unanswered and times out
with -ETIMEDOUT.

dev_err_probe() then treats that as a hard failure, so the pinctrl device
never probes again even once the ADSP is up. Everything downstream of it
stays in deferred probe forever, which on a Fairphone 5 leaves the whole
sound card unusable.

Translate the timeout into -EPROBE_DEFER so the probe is retried after the
ADSP has booted.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Marcus Ramberg <marcus@means.no>
---
diff --git a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c
index 76aed3296279..06b6e57dfea7 100644
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c
@@ -500,8 +500,20 @@ int lpi_pinctrl_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return ret;
 
 	ret = clk_bulk_prepare_enable(MAX_LPI_NUM_CLKS, pctrl->clks);
-	if (ret)
+	if (ret) {
+		/*
+		 * The core/audio clocks are provided by the ADSP (through
+		 * q6prm over GLINK). If the ADSP has not booted yet, the
+		 * clock enable request times out instead of deferring.
+		 * Translate the timeout into a probe deferral so the probe
+		 * is retried once the ADSP is up, instead of failing
+		 * permanently and leaving the whole sound card stuck in
+		 * deferred probe.
+		 */
+		if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT)
+			ret = -EPROBE_DEFER;
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "Can't enable clocks\n");
+	}
 
 	pctrl->desc.pctlops = &lpi_gpio_pinctrl_ops;
 	pctrl->desc.pmxops = &lpi_gpio_pinmux_ops;
